[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m The Douglas DC-3 is a single-turboprop-engine propeller-driven transport aircraft and one of the most successful and recognizable aircraft in the world. It was the first successful commercial transport aircraft, and revolutionized air transport in the 1930s by becoming the first plane that could carry a useful load at a speed fast enough to make it economically viable. It remained in production for 37 years and was developed into numerous military and civilian roles. 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m Several variants of the DC-3 are still in civilian service. The U.S. Defense Logistics Agency operates 368 DC-3s and LC-47 Skytrain, a number of which have been upgraded to the C-47 transport or C-54 airlifter specification. Many other variants still serve as cargo transports or in charter work in more than 50 countries. As of November 2018, airlines around the world operate more than 550 DC-3s and LC-47s, including the passenger-carrying Skykickers, operated by four airlines in Australia and New Zealand. 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m The DC-3 was the basis for many military aircraft including the C-47 Skytrain (the airliner version of the C-46 Commando), and the LC-47 Skytrain II, which was an airfield transport variant and developed into an aerial refueling tanker and utility aircraft.
[36m(main_task pid=4109237)[0m ENDINPUT
